### How the fourteen data sessions work

*Every Thursday takes **one source of data** and goes behind a number you have
already seen. Each session asks the same five questions — **what is one row? who
made this, and why? how do you get it? what can it settle? what can it never
settle?** — and each has its own reading, due before class, about that data.*

**The sessions come in six parts, and each part is a place data comes from.**
Not a topic — a *source*. Turnout turns up in four different parts of this
course and means something slightly different each time, because a different
organisation produced it for a different reason. Sorting by topic would hide
that. Sorting by where it came from is what makes it visible.

**Part I is the Census Bureau**, one agency that counts people and places. It
goes first because nearly every other part borrows a number from it: if you want
to know what share of a county voted, somebody has to tell you how many people
live there.

**Part II is surveys** — asking a sample of people questions and using the
answers to describe everyone. You will already have met one in Part I, because
the American Community Survey is a survey, and answering it is required by law.
Here you meet the voluntary kind, and examine what goes wrong with them.

**Part III is elections**, which is two things sharing a name: the **result**
that officials certified, and the **machinery** that produced it — who was
registered, who was removed, how many ballots were rejected.

**Part IV is what political actors leave behind.** Every member of Congress votes
on the record; every campaign files what it raised. The person in the row chose
to run for office.

**Part V is what ordinary people leave behind** — a traffic stop, a jury
summons, a grade stamped on a neighbourhood in 1937. Same kind of record, and
nobody in it volunteered.

**Part VI puts them together**, which is where the interesting questions and
nearly all the wrong answers live.

| Part | | Sessions | What opens it |
|---|---|---|---|
| **I** | The Census Bureau | 1–3 | *What a census is, and how you get it* |
| **II** | Surveys | 4–5 | *What a survey can establish, and how you get one* |
| **III** | Elections | 6–8, 10, 11 | *What a return is, and where you get one* |
| **IV** | Records of Political Actors | 9, 12 | *Filed because somebody had to* |
| **V** | Records of Ordinary People | 12 | *A record nobody asked you for* |
| **VI** | Putting Data Together | 13–14 | *A number somebody built* |

**The order is cumulative: nothing is inferred from a source you have not
already met.** The census (Part I) comes before the districts drawn on top of it.
Surveys (Part II) come before the models built out of them. Returns (Part III)
come before we ask who cast them, and the voter file — the other half of the
same part — comes before we guess race from it. And the last part is last because a score can only be graded
against data you already trust — which by then you do.

**One part is split on purpose.** Part IV runs twice: the money half in
**Session 9**, one week before the election, and the voting-record half in
Session 12. Money moves early because campaign finance and media attention are
worth teaching while the campaign is still running — the returns arrive six days
later — and because nothing in Part IV depends on Part III. It also puts polls
and forecasts (Session 5) and the money (Session 9) ahead of **election night
(Session 10)**, so the night grades work you have already done.

**One part arrives twice.** `election-night` is a Part II chapter that runs on
**Thu, Nov 5** and no other day, because it is built around returns that do not
exist until Nov 3. Session 10 is Part II arriving five weeks after the rest of
it, and the session is better for it: you met the midterm base rate in Session 5
and you grade it here against what the country actually did.

**Thirteen short labs sit on Tuesdays instead.** A chapter does not need the
whole eighty minutes, and thirteen of this term's labs earn their place by being
read rather than run: you read the *brief* — the written walkthrough, ten
numbered steps, no code — on your own, and we spend twenty to thirty minutes of a
Tuesday on it. They are marked **Tuesday companion** below. Most ask what a chart
does to data you have already met; the rest carry an argument, and two of those
arrive before the session they set up, on purpose — *One Ballot, One Number* on
the first day, because it is the question every later session inherits, and *Draw
the Line Somewhere Else*, which you meet in Week 2 and reread before the
capstone. **This does not add a fifteenth data session and it does not lengthen
Thursdays.** It uses time the chapter was not using.

**The textbook runs on its own track.** It is not tied to the parts, and it is
not read in the book's own order. Chapters are placed where they do the most
good — Ch. 2 with apportionment, Ch. 5 and 10 with the careers session, Ch. 4
and 7 with campaign money the week before the election, Ch. 13 with the polls.
Two consequences worth knowing in advance: Chapters 10 and 11 refer back to
Chapter 4 on campaign finance, which you read in **late October**, and several
chapters refer to Chapter 6 on parties, which you read in **November**. Neither
reference is load-bearing; read past them. **The three tests cover the textbook
and nothing else.**

