# The Four Standards: A Reference Sheet

***

This course evaluates everything against the same four standards. They are not
mine — they are the ones Sides et al. set out in Chapter 1 and then apply,
chapter by chapter, in the sections titled "Evaluating…". Learning them once
means you can evaluate any electoral arrangement we meet, including the many the
textbook never discusses.

**The standards conflict**, and that is the useful part. Almost every reform
that serves one of them costs you another, so an argument concluding "we should
have more of all four" has not engaged with the trade. When you meet a rule, an
institution, or a reform in this course, the question worth asking is which
standard it serves, which it sacrifices, and whether any data could tell you the
size of the sacrifice.

## 1. Free choice

*Roughly:* voters need a real choice, a hand in deciding what the choices are,
and freedom from coercion in making it.

Watch for: whether the choice set was fixed before you got there. Primaries,
nomination rules, ballot access, and two-party dominance all decide what is on
the menu, and turnout on election day cannot fix a menu already set.

## 2. Political equality

*Roughly:* every vote should carry equal weight, the costs of voting should fall
equally on everyone, and candidates should be able to reach voters on
comparable terms.

Watch for: the gap between formal and effective equality. One person one vote is
a rule about counting; whether your vote *matters* depends on apportionment,
districting, and where you happen to live. Sessions 1, 2, 3, 7 and 14 all live
here — Session 1 because its Tuesday companion sets out the inference across the
secret ballot that everything the courts are told about racially polarized
voting depends on, and Session 14 twice over, since the unit a district is drawn
from decides the answer before anybody argues about it.

## 3. Deliberation

*Roughly:* voters need enough information, from enough different sources, and
candidates should have to give reasons for their positions.

Watch for: volume mistaken for quality. Modern campaigns produce vastly more
information than any previous era while arguably narrowing what any given voter
sees. More speech is not automatically more deliberation.

## 4. Free speech

*Roughly:* government may not restrict political speech, and parties,
candidates and groups must be able to say what they want.

Watch for: this is the standard that most often blocks the fix. Nearly every
proposal to equalise campaign resources or improve deliberation runs into it.
*Buckley v. Valeo* is the hinge, and Chapter 4 is where we take it up.

